{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And this.</b> The Gaon [Saadia] says that we find no silver in the Tabernacle except the silver of the congregation's census. If so, how is it written that the silver was a donation? He answered that it was other silver, donated for the need of a silver table, for it is impossible that there be no table there to prepare anything upon, whether to cut or to wash upon, and for bowls and basins. In my opinion there is no need, for Scripture did not bother to exclude one item from the sixteen, since the fifteen were donations. Similarly: \"These are the sons of Jacob who were born to him in Paddan-aram\" (Genesis 35:26) — but Benjamin was born only in the land of Canaan. And further: \"with seventy persons your fathers went down\" (Deuteronomy 10:22) — but the sons of Joseph did not go down, for they were born there. He mentioned at the beginning of the donation the most precious, which is gold, and concluded with the most precious: shoham stones and setting stones, for these were found only among the princes, unlike gold, which is found among the multitude. Now there was no iron in the making of the Tabernacle, and concerning the First Temple it is written: \"hammers and the axe, any iron tool, were not heard in the House while it was being built\" (1 Kings 6:7)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The phrase לחתוך או לרחוץ עליו is attributed to the Gaon's answer; the translation preserves the syntactic ambiguity of where his explanation ends."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:38:11.520158966Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}